NAME
    grep - print the lines that match a regular expression

SYNOPSIS
    grep regex [file ...]
    grep -r regex [dir]

DESCRIPTION
    Prints the lines that match a basic regular expression. The input is
    the files named, read as one stream (globs expand, "-" is stdin), or
    stdin. With more than one file each matching line is prefixed
    "NAME:". grep is /BIN/GREP. stdin is the shell's < file or pipe (see
    shell), else the console up to Ctrl-D.

    With -r grep searches every file under the current directory (or
    dir), depth first, and prefixes each match with the file's absolute
    path: "PATH:line".

    The regex is the first word: a space ends it, and no quotes are
    stripped. The dialect (see regex):
        .         any single character
        c* c+ c?  zero or more / one or more / zero or one of the
                  character (or .) before it
        ^         the start of the line (only as the first character)
        $         the end of the line (only as the last character)
    Everything else is a literal: no classes, escapes or alternation.

OPTIONS
    -r    search a directory tree (lower-case r only)

LIMITS
    The regex is cut at 63 characters and lines at 255. Up to 24 file
    names, after glob expansion. -r descends 8 levels and says "grep:
    deeper than 8 levels, not all searched" when it stopped short; a
    file whose path is 64 characters or longer is skipped.

EXAMPLES
    grep ^a /FRUIT.TXT        the lines starting with "a"
    grep e.l /FRUIT.TXT       e, any character, l: "yellow"
    grep red$ /FRUIT*.TXT     both files, "/FRUIT.TXT:apple 3 red"...
    grep -r grep /MAN         every page that mentions grep
    grep apple                the console, up to Ctrl-D

NOTES
    Each file ends a line, so the last line of one file never joins the
    first line of the next.
    Removed from the P8X version: input from a pipe or a < redirect.
    Changed: -r takes a start directory and has no 36-file limit; any
    number of files (up to 24), with the NAME: prefix.
    Ported from P8X os/commands/grep.c 2026-09-23.

